Absolutely. And I think this one should be a design-direction document, not another technical specification.

It should tell the rebuilding AI how to think about Hess's UI/UX, what to borrow from Chess.com/iOS/other products, what not to copy, and how to design around Hess's unusual mechanics.

HESS UPDATE V3

UI/UX & Visual Design Direction

Design Reference & Experience Specification

Document Type: Product Design / UI/UX Direction
Status: Authoritative design direction for V3 rebuild
Rule Status: UI/UX may evolve during implementation
Relationship to other V3 documents: This document governs the experience and presentation of Hess, while the Rules/Engineering documents govern the underlying game system.


---

1. Purpose

Hess V3 should receive a complete UI/UX redesign, not merely a visual refresh of the existing interface.

The original Hess implementation intentionally used a relatively generic game interface. That was appropriate for proving the game itself, but V3 should treat the interface as part of the product.

The goal is to create an experience that feels:

polished

modern

responsive

understandable

game-focused

mobile-friendly

distinctive to Hess

capable of handling Hess's unusual mechanics without overwhelming the player


The design should take inspiration from established products and interaction systems, especially:

Chess.com and other high-quality chess interfaces

iOS / Apple Human Interface Guidelines

modern mobile games

high-quality board-game interfaces

established interaction patterns for tutorials, analysis, menus, and contextual actions


These references are sources of design principles, not templates to copy.

> Hess's rules are original. Its interface should also develop its own identity.




---

2. Core Design Principle

The most important design principle is:

> Design the interface around Hess, rather than forcing Hess into a conventional chess interface.



Chess provides useful familiarity:

board

pieces

turns

captures

move history

player information

game states

analysis


But Hess introduces additional concepts:

hidden setup

unusual movement systems

King Swap

Queen Desperation Raid

Jester lifecycle

Jester sacrifice decisions

special movement continuations

unusual endgame conditions

two-King countdown

complex positional consequences


The interface therefore needs to support a game that looks understandable at first glance but becomes deeper as the player discovers it.


---

3. Design Reference Philosophy

The implementation agent should actively study successful interfaces before designing V3.

However:

Do NOT do this

> "Make Hess look like Chess.com."



or:

> "Make Hess look like iOS."



Instead:

> "Identify what these systems do exceptionally well, understand why those interactions work, and adapt the principles to Hess."




---

4. Chess.com as a Reference

Chess.com should primarily be treated as a reference for gameplay interaction, rather than visual branding.

Useful areas to study:

Board interaction

Study:

selecting pieces

highlighting legal moves

capture indication

last-move indication

check/check-like states where applicable

board orientation

touch interaction

drag vs tap interaction

move confirmation

animation timing


Hess should have an equally clear interaction language, while accounting for its own movement systems.

Game information

Study how chess interfaces communicate:

whose turn it is

player identity

game state

move history

captured pieces

game completion

resign/draw/options

analysis/review


Hess should adopt similarly strong information hierarchy.

Move history

The move list should become more useful in Hess because special actions can make ordinary notation insufficient.

The interface should visually distinguish things such as:

normal movement

captures

King Swaps

promotions

Jester-related actions

special continuation actions

other major state transitions


The exact notation remains an engine concern, but the UI should make special actions immediately recognizable.


---

5. iOS-Inspired Interaction Design

Hess should use iOS-inspired interaction principles, especially for mobile.

This does not mean copying Apple's interface.

Useful principles include:

Hierarchy

Important information should be immediately obvious.

Secondary information should remain available without competing with the board.

Progressive disclosure

Do not explain every Hess rule simultaneously.

Show information when it becomes relevant.

For example:

Normal game

> Your turn



rather than a giant explanation of every possible Hess mechanic.

When a player encounters a Jester decision:

> Jester reached a sacrifice point
Choose what happens next.



The relevant information appears when the player needs it.

Sheets and contextual actions

Special decisions can use lightweight sheets, popovers, or contextual panels rather than navigating away from the game.

For example:

Jester decision

Sacrifice
Return
Cancel

while keeping the board visible behind the decision.

The exact interaction should be determined by usability testing.

Touch targets

Controls must be comfortable on phones.

Avoid:

tiny icons

tightly packed controls

precision-dependent buttons

controls that require repeated tapping


Hess should remain playable comfortably on a small Android screen.


---

6. Mobile-First, Not Mobile-Reduced

The primary design target should be a phone.

Desktop should then expand the experience rather than define it.

A mobile player should be able to:

understand the board

select pieces

make moves

perform special actions

understand whose turn it is

access move history

access rules/help

interact with AI

exit/pause/review the game


without fighting the interface.

The board should remain the visual center of the game.


---

7. The Board Is the Product's Center

The game screen should not feel like a dashboard with a chessboard embedded inside it.

The hierarchy should generally be:

Game state

↓

Board

↓

Relevant contextual controls

↓

Secondary information

The board should receive the majority of the player's visual attention.

Avoid filling the game screen with:

unnecessary cards

excessive statistics

decorative panels

large instructional text

permanent explanations

unnecessary buttons



---

8. Hess Visual Identity

The design agent should develop a visual language specifically for Hess.

It should explore:

typography

board treatment

piece presentation

motion

surfaces

iconography

state indicators

transitions

sound/haptic opportunities

color semantics


The final result should feel like one coherent product, rather than:

> Chess.com board + iOS menus + random game UI.



Avoid generic AI/SaaS aesthetics

Do not automatically use:

purple gradients

excessive glassmorphism

glowing AI effects

generic rounded cards everywhere

excessive gradients

meaningless decorative blobs

emoji as UI icons


Icons should preferably use a consistent icon system such as Lucide or an equivalent professional icon library.


---

9. Board Design

The board should be visually distinctive but remain highly readable.

Important considerations:

Square clarity

Players should immediately understand:

selected square

legal destination

capture destination

last move

special movement

blocked movement

dangerous/critical states


Special movement visualization

This is particularly important for Hess.

A player should be able to understand unusual movement through the board itself.

For example, a Missile Rook should make its unusual movement pattern visually obvious.

The Staircase Bishop should communicate its staircase trajectory.

The T-Shape Knight should communicate its available routes.

The player should not always need to open the Rulebook to understand what happened.


---

10. Movement Feedback

Movement feedback should be informative rather than decorative.

Possible feedback:

subtle square highlighting

movement path previews

destination indicators

capture markers

short movement animations

special-action animation

haptic feedback on supported devices


Animations should communicate state transitions.

They should not delay gameplay.


---

11. Special Actions Need Their Own Interaction Language

Hess contains actions that aren't ordinary piece movement.

These should not be visually indistinguishable from normal moves.

Examples:

King Swap

The UI should communicate:

> This is a swap, not a normal King move.



The player should clearly see:

the King

eligible pawn(s)

the swap relationship

the resulting positions


Queen Desperation Raid

The UI should communicate:

that the special condition has activated

how many enemy-territory moves remain

when the Queen returns

what restrictions currently apply


Jester

Jester interactions may involve multiple states.

The UI should clearly communicate:

current Jester state

movement direction/state

available actions

sacrifice decision

transformation/return decisions

continuation of the turn when applicable


The player should never wonder:

> "Why did the game suddenly give me another decision?"



The UI should explain the state succinctly.


---

12. Resolution States

Hess contains situations where a normal move is not immediately sufficient to resolve the game state.

For V3, these should be treated as explicit Resolution States.

This is a provisional engineering/design term.

Examples:

Jester sacrifice decision

Jester home-rank decision

special continuation

other future mechanics requiring player choice


The UI should make the state unmistakable.

Example:

> Jester Decision
Your Jester has reached a sacrifice point.



Then present only the currently legal choices.

Do not expose impossible choices and then display an error after selection.


---

13. Piece Academy

The Piece Academy should be one of the strongest parts of Hess's onboarding.

It should contain:

1. Interaction Mode

A player can freely interact with pieces.

They should be able to:

select pieces

move them

test movement

place pieces where appropriate

experiment with interactions


This is important because Hess is fundamentally a game that benefits from experimentation.

The player should be able to discover:

> "What happens if I put this piece here?"



rather than only reading instructions.


---

2. Piece Lessons

Structured lessons explain individual pieces.

The lesson should demonstrate the movement visually.

Avoid walls of text.

Prefer:

Visual demonstration

→

short explanation

→

player tries it

→

small challenge


---

3. Special Mechanics

Complex interactions should be taught separately.

Examples:

Hidden Setup

King Swap

Queen Raid

Jester lifecycle

Desperation Stale

Two-King countdown



---

4. Guided Practice Match

A simplified match against an easy AI.

The game should introduce mechanics progressively.

When the player encounters an unfamiliar mechanic for the first time, pause or slow the experience enough to explain it.


---

14. Contextual Help

Hess should avoid forcing players to memorize the entire ruleset.

A player should be able to access contextual information while playing.

For example:

Long-pressing a piece could show a compact Piece Card containing:

piece name

small movement visualization

short description

relevant special state


This should be concise.

It should not open a giant documentation page unless requested.


---

15. Rulebook

The Rulebook should be designed for reference, not required reading before playing.

Recommended structure:

Basic Rules

board

turn order

basic movement

captures

objective


Advanced Situations

King Swap

Desperation Stale

Two-King countdown

other unusual game-state rules


Legendary Pieces

Jester

Queen Raid

other complex mechanics


The player should be able to jump directly to what they need.


---

16. Setup Experience

Hidden Setup is one of Hess's major differences from ordinary chess.

Therefore setup should feel deliberate and exciting rather than like a configuration form.

The opponent's hidden configuration should remain genuinely hidden.

The interface can communicate:

> Your setup



and separately:

> Opponent is preparing



without revealing the opponent's choices.

Once both players are ready:

> Setup revealed



Then transition into the game.

This reveal can be a meaningful animation, but it should remain short.


---

17. Information Privacy Is Also UX

Security and UX overlap in Hess.

If setup is supposed to be hidden, the interface must never accidentally reveal it through:

animations

move previews

network state

opponent-facing UI

notifications

temporary board states

cached data


The user experience should reflect the actual privacy model.


---

18. Game States Must Be Visually Obvious

The interface should clearly communicate states such as:

Your turn

Opponent's turn

AI thinking

special decision pending

game won

game lost

draw

disconnected

reconnecting

opponent disconnected

setup pending

setup revealed


Avoid relying purely on color.

Use combinations of:

text

iconography

position

animation

semantic color where appropriate



---

19. AI Experience

Hess's AI should feel like a legitimate opponent.

The UI should not pretend the AI is a human.

During AI thinking, provide subtle feedback such as:

> Hess is thinking…



Avoid excessive fake activity.

For development/debug builds, provide optional AI reasoning information such as:

candidate moves

evaluation

search depth

principal variation

selected move

evaluation components


This information does not necessarily need to be visible to normal players.

But the architecture should make the AI observable during development.


---

20. Difficulty

Difficulty should primarily change:

search depth

evaluation sophistication

computational budget

tactical awareness

strategic planning


It should not primarily mean:

> "Make random bad moves."



Easy AI can play simpler strategies, but it must remain rule-correct.

A player should never encounter an AI that:

makes illegal moves

gets stuck

cannot resolve a special action

freezes because there are no ordinary moves

misunderstands Jester continuation

repeatedly shuffles without understanding the game state because the search system cannot evaluate it



---

21. Motion Design

Animation should communicate meaning.

Good animation:

piece movement

capture

swap

promotion

Jester transformation

setup reveal

special activation

game completion


Bad animation:

decorative effects everywhere

excessive bouncing

long transitions

effects that obscure the board

animations that make the player wait unnecessarily


The game should feel alive without feeling noisy.


---

22. Sound and Haptics

These should be considered optional enhancement layers.

Possible haptic events:

selecting a piece

successful move

capture

special action

promotion

major game-state change


Sound should similarly distinguish important events.

However:

> No feedback effect should be required to understand the game.




---

23. Accessibility

The design must support players who cannot rely on subtle color differences.

Consider:

sufficient contrast

readable typography

touch target sizes

semantic labels

screen-reader-friendly controls

reduced-motion support

non-color indicators for game states

clear focus states where relevant


Special mechanics should remain understandable without animation alone.


---

24. Responsive Layout

Phone

Prioritize:

Player → Board → Essential controls

Tablet

Allow additional information beside/below the board where useful.

Desktop

Potentially support:

board

move history

game information

contextual analysis

optional panels


But desktop should not create unnecessary complexity.


---

25. Navigation Architecture

The application should have a small number of clear destinations.

Possible high-level structure:

Play

New Game

Play vs AI

Multiplayer

Continue/Reconnect


Learn

Piece Academy

Special Mechanics

Guided Match

Rulebook


Review

Game history/replay

Analysis


Settings

Board preferences

Sound

Haptics

Accessibility

Other preferences


The exact navigation structure may change after implementation testing.

Do not create navigation categories simply because conventional apps have them.


---

26. Game Screen Should Stay Focused

The game screen should not become a control center.

Permanent controls should be limited to actions players actually need during the game.

Secondary options can live behind:

menus

sheets

contextual controls


The player's attention should remain on the game.


---

27. No Timer by Default

Hess currently does not require a chess-style clock.

The interface should therefore not assume that a timer is necessary.

If a timer is introduced later, it should be added as an explicit game mode rather than embedded into the fundamental Hess experience.


---

28. Error States

Errors should explain what happened.

Bad:

> Invalid action.



Better:

> That pawn has already moved, so it cannot use its starting double-step.



Even better when possible:

> Unavailable
Starting double-step is only available to an unmoved pawn.



This is particularly important during development and Academy mode.


---

29. Empty and Edge States

The implementation should explicitly design:

no legal moves

no eligible Jester sacrifice

no available King Swap

disconnected opponent

reconnecting

incomplete setup

completed setup

game already ended

expired session

AI unable to continue

unexpected engine state


The UI must never simply freeze.

Every engine state should have a defined UI representation or a controlled error/recovery path.


---

30. Design System

The rebuild should establish reusable design primitives rather than styling each screen independently.

Define reusable components for:

buttons

icon buttons

sheets

cards

status indicators

player headers

piece cards

rule cards

move history entries

dialogs

game-state banners

tutorial overlays

special-action controls

navigation

typography

spacing

animation timing

board states


This prevents the V3 interface from becoming another collection of unrelated screens.


---

31. Icons

Use a consistent icon system.

Preferred:

Lucide

another professional vector icon library


Avoid using emojis as primary interface icons.

Icons should communicate actions consistently throughout the application.


---

32. What the Design Agent Must NOT Do

The implementation agent must not:

blindly reproduce the existing Hess UI

blindly reproduce Chess.com

blindly reproduce iOS

turn Hess into a generic SaaS dashboard

fill every surface with cards

use decorative UI instead of functional feedback

hide important game state behind unnecessary menus

explain every rule permanently

rely entirely on color

use animation to compensate for unclear UX

sacrifice board readability for aesthetics

introduce visual complexity simply because Hess has complex rules



---

33. Design Exploration Is Expected

The first implementation does not need to be the final visual design.

The agent should be allowed to explore alternatives for:

board presentation

game controls

special-action UI

setup

Academy

Rulebook

navigation

AI status

mobile layout


However, exploration must remain grounded in the established product principles.


---

34. Prototype the Most Difficult Screen First

The most important design prototype is not the home screen.

It is the actual Hess gameplay screen.

The agent should test the design against:

1. normal movement


2. capture


3. King Swap


4. Jester interaction


5. Queen Raid


6. hidden setup


7. promotion


8. unusual endgame state


9. AI thinking


10. mobile touch interaction



If the interface handles those elegantly, simpler screens will be much easier.


---

35. Design Validation Questions

During implementation, repeatedly ask:

Clarity

> Can a new player understand what is happening without reading a manual?
Discoverability
Can a player discover Hess's unusual mechanics naturally?
Control
Does the player always know what actions are currently available?
Feedback
Does every meaningful action produce understandable feedback?
Density
Is the interface showing too much information?
Identity
Does this feel like Hess rather than generic chess?
Mobile usability
Can this comfortably be used with one hand on a phone?
Complexity
Is the UI handling Hess's complexity, or merely exposing that complexity to the player?
That last question is especially important.
36. Design Philosophy in One Sentence
The target experience is:
The familiarity of a polished chess interface, the clarity of a well-designed mobile app, and the personality of an original strategy game.
37. Final Directive to the Implementation Agent
Do not treat this document as a request to decorate the existing Hess application.
Treat it as permission to rethink the entire presentation layer.
The existing Hess UI should be inspected for useful behavior and discarded where appropriate.
Chess platforms such as Chess.com should be studied for excellent game interaction.
iOS should be studied for hierarchy, navigation, touch interaction, contextual controls, sheets, animation, and information architecture.
Modern board games and mobile games should be studied for onboarding, feedback, special mechanics, and interaction design.
But the final product must be Hess.
The objective is not imitation.
The objective is to take proven interaction ideas and combine them into a coherent interface designed specifically for Hess's rules, mechanics, and players.
Gameplay clarity comes before visual novelty.
Discoverability comes before documentation.
Usability comes before decoration.
Hess's identity comes from how its unique systems are experienced, not from copying another product's appearance.
V3 Design Success Condition
The redesign succeeds when a player can look at Hess and think:
“I understand how to use this.”
Then, after encountering the unusual mechanics:
“Oh… this game is much deeper than I thought.”
—not:
“Where am I supposed to click?”
and not:
“Why does this look like a copy of Chess.com?”
This document should be included with the other Hess V3 documents when the rebuild agent receives the project specification.
